[Variation "Why Black attacks d4 from the flank"]

{ Volume 1: The Sicilian from First Principles. Why it matters: Black does not mirror 1.e4. The c-pawn challenges d4 while preserving the e-pawn, creating an unbalanced center and genuine winning chances. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 { Nc3: White reroutes a knight to c3; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Finish development without surrendering d5, use the half-open c-file, and choose ...e6 or ...e5 only when its square concessions are understood. White plan: White uses the lead in space and development to build pressure before Black's queenside assets become active. Decision: After the c-pawn exchange, which central square needs protection and which file became Black's long-term highway? Danger: Playing thematic pawn moves automatically while the king and queenside pieces remain undeveloped. Memory rule: 1...c5 buys asymmetry; Black must pay for it with accurate development. } *

