[Variation "The half-open c-file bargain"]

{ Volume 1: The Sicilian from First Principles. Why it matters: The exchange on d4 gives White a central pawn majority but gives Black a half-open c-file and targets on c2 and the queenside. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 d6 6. Be2 g6 { g6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. O-O Bg7 { Bg7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Coordinate queen and rook on the c-file, pressure c2, and make White spend time guarding entry squares. White plan: White seeks central expansion or a kingside initiative before c-file pressure becomes concrete. Decision: Is the c-file currently an attacking line, or must development and a pawn break open access first? Danger: Confusing possession of a half-open file with control of its entry squares. Memory rule: A file matters only when pieces can enter it with tempo. } *

