[Variation "Development before decoration"]

{ Volume 1: The Sicilian from First Principles. Why it matters: Najdorf flexibility is valuable only after Black survives the development race; ...a6 is preparation, not permission to drift. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Develop, castle, and only then decide whether queenside expansion or central action is justified. White plan: White tries to exploit every slow move with f4, Be3, or a central break while Black's king is still exposed. Decision: Can Black complete one useful developing move before starting the wing operation? Danger: Collecting thematic moves such as ...a6, ...b5, and ...Qc7 without checking tactical readiness. Memory rule: In the Sicilian, a slow move is safe only when it supports the next forcing event. } *

