{ Volume 2: The Three Sicilian Centers. Why it matters: Pawns on d6 and e6 form a compact shell. Black concedes space temporarily in exchange for flexible ...d5 and ...b5 breaks. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 e6 6. Be2 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 7. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Complete development, watch e5 and d5, and time ...d5 when exchanges release the cramped pieces. White plan: White adds pressure with f4, Qf3, Be3, and sometimes g4 before Black equalizes with a break. Decision: Does ...d5 work tactically now, or does one white piece still overload the d5 square? Danger: Remaining passive because the shell looks safe; without a break the space deficit becomes permanent. Memory rule: The small center is a spring: defend it, then release it with ...d5. } *

