[Variation "The Najdorf ...e5 clamp"]

{ Volume 2: The Three Sicilian Centers. Why it matters: ...e5 gains time on the knight and controls d4, but it permanently weakens d5 and leaves d6 backward. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. Be3 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Control d5 with ...Be6, ...Nbd7, and rooks on central files; seek ...d5 only when preparation makes it possible. White plan: White installs a knight on d5, trades the defender on f6, or attacks d6 after fixing Black's structure. Decision: What is the concrete plan for the d5 hole before committing the e-pawn to e5? Danger: Admiring the space gain while allowing Nd5 under favorable circumstances. Memory rule: ...e5 gains a tempo but creates a square; every gain sends a bill. } *

