{ Volume 2: The Three Sicilian Centers. Why it matters: Many Sicilians are strategically organized around one question: can Black achieve ...d5 without losing material or king safety? } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. Qd2 b5 8. f3 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. g4 h6 10. O-O-O Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 11. Kb1 Rc8 { Rc8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Improve control of d5, distract White on the c-file or queenside, and break only when the resulting exchanges activate the pieces. White plan: White restrains ...d5, keeps more space, and launches first on the kingside. Decision: After ...d5, can White capture, push, or exploit an opened diagonal with tempo? Danger: Playing ...d5 because it is thematic without calculating every forcing capture. Memory rule: A freeing break is earned by control, not by hope. } *

