{ Volume 3: Map of the Sicilian Families. Why it matters: The Dragon develops the bishop in one move and attacks the center from g7, accepting a sharp race against queenside castling. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 { Bg7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Use the g7 bishop, c-file, and tactical exchange sacrifices to attack before White opens the h-file. White plan: White builds the Yugoslav setup, castles long, and pushes h4-h5 to strip the king. Decision: Whose forcing attack lands first, and which exchange changes the race clock? Danger: Playing on the queenside too slowly while White's pawn storm opens lines with tempo. Memory rule: The Dragon is a race; count forcing moves, not attractive moves. } *

