{ Volume 3: Map of the Sicilian Families. Why it matters: ...e5 and ...b5 seize space and activity, while d5 and d6 become lasting strategic targets. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 { b5: Black starts queenside expansion and prepares files or a bishop diagonal. Black plan: Use dynamic piece play and timely ...f5 before White consolidates on the weak squares. White plan: White occupies d5, pressures d6, and tries to convert structural weaknesses after exchanges. Decision: Does Black's next active move create a concrete threat before White fixes the weaknesses? Danger: Entering the structure without accepting that passive defense will simply lose the weak pawns. Memory rule: The Sveshnikov trades squares for time; spend the time immediately. } *

