{ Volume 3: Map of the Sicilian Families. Why it matters: The Taimanov delays ...d6 and develops flexibly, preserving several central structures and move-order tricks. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 { a6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O Bb4 { Bb4: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Pressure c3, choose ...Nf6 or ...Nge7 intelligently, and use ...b5 or ...d5 according to White's setup. White plan: White seeks fast long castling, f3-g4, or a Maroczy-style bind before Black defines the center. Decision: Which white setup has appeared, and which flexible option should now be converted into a concrete plan? Danger: Keeping every option open so long that no piece receives a definite job. Memory rule: Flexibility is useful only when you know the trigger that ends it. } *

