{ Volume 4: Najdorf Foundations and Move Orders. Why it matters: ...e6 keeps d5 flexible and supports ...d5; ...e5 gains space and tempo but creates a permanent d5 outpost. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. f4 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Select the center from concrete demands: piece placement, White's pressure, and whether d5 can be controlled. White plan: White chooses plans that expose the price of the center: a space squeeze against ...e6 or Nd5 against ...e5. Decision: Which structure gives your pieces active squares while denying White a favorable break? Danger: Choosing the familiar pawn move without checking whether White's setup was designed to punish it. Memory rule: The central pawn decides the whole position; choose it after reading the pieces. } *

