{ Volume 4: Najdorf Foundations and Move Orders. Why it matters: Queenside expansion is useful when it gains space, drives a piece, supports ...Bb7, or creates a file; otherwise it may expose b6 and a6. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. O-O Be7 8. f4 Qc7 { Qc7: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. } 9. Kh1 O-O 10. a4 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Prepare ...b5 with piece control, king safety, and an answer to e5 or a4; use ...b4 only when the knight displacement matters. White plan: White restrains with a4 or opens the center while Black spends tempi on pawns. Decision: If ...b5 is played, what concrete improvement arrives on the next move? Danger: Treating ...b5 as a compulsory Najdorf badge rather than a calculated operation. Memory rule: A wing pawn move must buy time, a square, or a file. } *

