{ Volume 5: Najdorf English Attack. Why it matters: White's pieces and pawns point at the kingside while Black's queenside play targets the castled king; development speed sets the race clock. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. f3 b5 8. Qd2 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. g4 h6 10. O-O-O Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use ...b5, ...Bb7, ...Nbd7, and the c-file, while keeping ...d5 or ...h5 as tactical brakes. White plan: White pushes g4-g5 and h4-h5, opens files, and often sacrifices material to expose Black's king. Decision: Which forcing move changes the race now: a central break, a pawn contact, or a direct threat to the king? Danger: Making a quiet improving move when the opponent already has a forcing sequence. Memory rule: In opposite-side attacks, count checks, captures, and pawn contacts. } *

