{ Volume 5: Najdorf English Attack. Why it matters: ...Ng4 challenges the key e3 bishop before White finishes the attacking setup, but it spends tempi and loosens dark squares. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 { Ng4: Black reroutes a knight to g4; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 { g5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 9. Bg3 Bg7 { Bg7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use the gained bishop tempo to seize kingside space, then return to development before the advanced pawns become targets. White plan: White preserves the bishop, attacks the pawn chain, and exploits weakened f5, h5, and dark squares. Decision: Does the bishop chase win useful structure or merely move the same knight and pawns repeatedly? Danger: Continuing the pawn chase after its strategic purpose is complete. Memory rule: Disrupt the setup, then develop; harassment is not a development plan. } *

