{ Volume 5: Najdorf English Attack. Why it matters: Black accepts d5 in exchange for central space and a tempo on the knight, producing a slower race with precise square control. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be6 8. f3 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. g4 Be7 10. Qd2 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Control d5, complete development, and use ...b5 while watching whether ...d5 can ever be prepared. White plan: White uses Nd5, g5, and queenside castling to combine an outpost with a direct attack. Decision: Can White occupy d5 under favorable exchanges, and which black piece must prevent it? Danger: Launching queenside play while d5 and d6 collapse in the center. Memory rule: In the ...e5 structure, d5 is the alarm bell for every plan. } *

