{ Volume 6: Najdorf 6.Bg5. Why it matters: Once White castles long, ...b5 gains tactical force, but central contact can open lines against either king before the pawn storm arrives. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. f4 Be7 8. Qf3 Qc7 { Qc7: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. } 9. O-O-O b5 10. e5 Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Combine ...b5 with ...Bb7 and calculate e5; open files only when Black's king has a defensible destination. White plan: White strikes e5 or f5 to make the center explode while Black's king remains undecided. Decision: Is the wing attack faster than White's central forcing line? Danger: Assuming opposite-side castling makes every pawn push automatically correct. Memory rule: The center can end a wing race before the pawns reach their targets. } *

