{ Volume 7: Positional Najdorf Systems. Why it matters: White develops without committing the king to a race, then pressures d5 and may expand with f4. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. Be3 Be6 10. f4 { f4: White claims kingside space and announces e5 or f5 attacking ideas. Black plan: Secure d5, coordinate rooks, and choose queenside play only after the center is stable. White plan: White uses Nd5, f4-f5, and pressure on d6 in a controlled positional squeeze. Decision: Which exchange reduces White's control of d5 without creating a new weakness? Danger: Playing as if a direct attack is required when patient central defense is enough. Memory rule: Against Be2, solve d5 first and attack second. } *

