{ Volume 7: Positional Najdorf Systems. Why it matters: The fianchetto controls dark squares and supports a slower central game; White reduces tactical targets and keeps several pawn breaks available. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. g3 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nde2 Be7 8. Bg2 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. O-O b5 { b5: Black starts queenside expansion and prepares files or a bishop diagonal. Black plan: Take space with ...b5, control d5, and prevent White from achieving a cost-free f4 or Nd5 setup. White plan: White reroutes pieces, uses f4, and exploits the long diagonal once the center opens. Decision: Can Black improve queenside space without weakening the diagonal toward the king? Danger: Forcing tactics in a position whose main demand is patient square control. Memory rule: Quiet systems still contain a race, but the currency is squares rather than checks. } *

