{ Volume 10: Closed Sicilian Systems. Why it matters: ...d5 challenges White before the kingside buildup is complete and often turns the game into an open central struggle. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. g3 g6 4. Bg2 Bg7 { Bg7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 5. d3 e6 6. f4 Nge7 { Nge7: Black reroutes a knight to e7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 7. Nf3 d5 { d5: Black plays the freeing central break; its tactical soundness matters more than its thematic reputation. Black plan: Support the break, accept exchanges that free the pieces, and avoid leaving d5 as a loose target. White plan: White may e5, exd5, or keep tension, choosing the structure that best preserves kingside chances. Decision: After each white response to ...d5, which side benefits from the opened files and changed knight squares? Danger: Playing the correct break with insufficient support and losing the d5 pawn tactically. Memory rule: The best answer to a slow flank buildup is often a timely central question. } *

