[Variation "Accepting the gambit"]

{ Volume 14: Smith-Morra Gambit. Why it matters: Black takes a pawn but gives White open lines and rapid development. Survival depends on development, not on defending the extra pawn at all costs. } 1. e4 c5 2. d4 cxd4 { cxd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 3. c3 dxc3 4. Nxc3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nf3 d6 6. Bc4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 7. O-O e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. Black plan: Control d5 and e5, develop every piece, and return the pawn if it neutralizes White's initiative. White plan: White places rooks on central files, attacks e6 and f7, and uses sacrifices to keep Black uncastled. Decision: Which move develops with defense against White's next forcing threat? Danger: Spending tempi to preserve the c3 pawn or another material detail while the king stays central. Memory rule: In a gambit, extra material is stored value; spend it to finish development. } *

