{ Volume 14: Smith-Morra Gambit. Why it matters: ...e6 denies White an easy e5 advance and supports development, but it makes the dark squares and d6 setup important. } 1. e4 c5 2. d4 cxd4 { cxd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 3. c3 dxc3 4. Nxc3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nf3 e6 6. Bc4 a6 { a6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. O-O b5 8. Bb3 Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use ...a6 and ...b5 only when they gain a real bishop tempo and do not neglect ...Nf6 and king safety. White plan: White exploits the lead in development with e5, Qe2, Rd1, and tactical pressure on the king. Decision: Does the queenside expansion solve a development problem or create one? Danger: Winning tempi on one bishop while three black pieces remain asleep. Memory rule: A pawn gain survives only behind a completed development shell. } *

