{ Volume 14: Smith-Morra Gambit. Why it matters: Declining with ...Nf6 avoids the gambit pawn and transposes to an Alapin-type center where e5 and d4 are the targets. } 1. e4 c5 2. d4 cxd4 { cxd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 3. c3 Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 { Nd5: Black reroutes a knight to d5; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nf3 e6 6. cxd4 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. Bc4 dxe5 { dxe5: Black releases tension by capturing on e5; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Attack the advanced center, develop with tempo, and know that declining changes the problem rather than removing theory. White plan: White keeps space and uses quick development to support the center. Decision: Can Black undermine e5 or d4 without allowing a tactical jump on the centralized knight? Danger: Calling the gambit declined and then playing passively against the broad center. Memory rule: Declining a gambit means choosing a center you understand, not refusing to fight. } *

