{ Volume 15: Rare Second Moves and Move-Order Traps. Why it matters: Bc4 points at f7 but allows Black to challenge the center immediately because White has not prepared d4. } 1. e4 c5 2. Bc4 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. Nf3 d5 4. exd5 exd5 { exd5: Black releases tension by capturing on d5; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. Bb5+ Bd7 6. Qe2+ Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use ...e6 and ...d5 to gain central equality while developing against checks and pins. White plan: White creates tactical pressure on d5 and f7 and may use Bb5+ to interrupt development. Decision: Can ...d5 be played without losing the pawn or exposing the king to a forcing check? Danger: Treating Bc4 as harmless and making a slow Sicilian move while White builds d4 for free. Memory rule: Punish an early bishop move by claiming the center, but calculate the checks. } *

