{ Volume 15: Rare Second Moves and Move-Order Traps. Why it matters: White sacrifices a wing pawn to distract c5 and gain central development; Black must avoid a greedy pawn-holding operation. } 1. e4 c5 2. b4 cxb4 { cxb4: Black releases tension by capturing on b4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 3. a3 d5 4. exd5 Qxd5 { Qxd5: Black releases tension by capturing on d5; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. Nf3 e5 6. axb4 Bxb4 { Bxb4: Black releases tension by capturing on b4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Challenge the center with ...d5, return material if useful, and make the queen's development safe from tempi. White plan: White opens files, plays Nf3 and d4, and attacks the queen or uncastled king with rapid piece play. Decision: After accepting, what central move prevents White from building d4 with tempo? Danger: Protecting every extra pawn while White mobilizes all pieces. Memory rule: Meet a wing gambit in the center and value time above pawns. } *

