{ Volume 15: Rare Second Moves and Move-Order Traps. Why it matters: White delays d4 and may build a restrained center, hoping Black commits before the structure is clear. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. c3 Nf6 4. Be2 g6 { g6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 5. O-O Bg7 6. d3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 7. Re1 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Develop flexibly, keep ...d5 available, and do not force an Open Sicilian structure that White declined. White plan: White prepares d4 under ideal conditions or keeps a King's Indian Attack shell for kingside play. Decision: Is White still preparing d4, and which black move makes that break less favorable? Danger: Expanding on the queenside before identifying whether the center will open. Memory rule: Against a delayed center, preserve options and watch the break, not the opening label. } *

