[Variation "The Dragon exchange sacrifice on c3"]

{ Volume 16: Sicilian Tactical Grammar. Why it matters: ...Rxc3 motifs destroy the knight guarding e4 and the castled king, often trading exchange material for structure, dark squares, and attack. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 { Bg7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O Rc8 { Rc8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. } 11. Bb3 Ne5 12. h4 Nc4 { Nc4: Black reroutes a knight to c4; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 13. Bxc4 Rxc4 { Rxc4: Black releases tension by capturing on c4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Calculate ...Rxc3 only when the follow-up reaches c2, the king, or e4 before White consolidates. White plan: White protects c3 tactically, trades attacking pieces, and returns material to reach safety. Decision: After the exchange sacrifice, name the next two forcing moves and the exact compensation. Danger: Sacrificing because the pattern is famous when no queen entry or pawn destruction follows. Memory rule: A thematic sacrifice still needs a forcing continuation and countable compensation. } *

