[Variation "The Najdorf ...d5 clearance"]

{ Volume 16: Sicilian Tactical Grammar. Why it matters: A central ...d5 can clear diagonals, remove White's space, and turn queenside pressure into a direct attack. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. Qd2 b5 8. f3 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. g4 Bb7 10. O-O-O Rc8 { Rc8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. } 11. Kb1 Ne5 { Ne5: Black reroutes a knight to e5; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Prepare the break with piece pressure and calculate every central capture, especially discovered attacks on the kings. White plan: White keeps d5 controlled and uses g5 or e5 to seize the initiative first. Decision: What opens after ...d5: which rook, bishop, and queen lines appear for both sides? Danger: Evaluating only the pawn exchange and overlooking the lines it uncovers. Memory rule: A clearance break is about the pieces released, not the pawn traded. } *

