{ Volume 16: Sicilian Tactical Grammar. Why it matters: Opposite-side attacks become tactical when pawns make contact: g5, h5, ...b4, or ...d5 changes lines and tempos immediately. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. f3 b5 8. g4 h6 { h6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 9. Qd2 Nbd7 10. O-O-O Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 11. h4 { h4: White changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. Black plan: Identify the first profitable contact, calculate forcing replies, and use defensive pawn moves only when they slow more than they weaken. White plan: White opens h- or g-files and uses sacrifices to remove the last pawn shield. Decision: Which pawn contact is forcing, and does it create a line for a piece already aimed at the king? Danger: Counting squares advanced rather than files opened and tempi gained. Memory rule: Pawn storms are decided at contact, not by distance traveled. } *

