{ Volume 17: Defense, Endgames, and Transformations. Why it matters: When White's initiative depends on queen pressure, a queen trade can reveal that Black's space or bishop pair is sufficient for the endgame. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. Be3 Be6 10. Qd2 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 11. Rfd1 Rc8 { Rc8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Offer exchanges that remove attackers while preserving active rooks and control of d5. White plan: White avoids sterile trades and tries to retain pressure on d6 and the weak squares. Decision: Which exchange removes attacking potential without conceding a superior minor-piece ending? Danger: Trading automatically and entering a lost pawn ending or a permanent d5 bind. Memory rule: Trade the attacker, not the structure that holds your position together. } *

