{ Volume 17: Defense, Endgames, and Transformations. Why it matters: The c-file remains a durable asset after attacks fade; rooks can pressure c2 and enter through c4 or c1 if squares are controlled. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. O-O Be7 8. f4 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. Kh1 Qc7 10. a4 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 11. Be3 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Contest entry squares, improve the worst piece, and create a second target before invading. White plan: White blocks the file, activates centrally, and attacks d6 or queenside weaknesses before the rook enters. Decision: What is the rook's entry square, and which minor piece must control it first? Danger: Doubling rooks on a file with no target, entry square, or supporting piece. Memory rule: A file is a road; first identify the destination. } *

