{ Volume 17: Defense, Endgames, and Transformations. Why it matters: The d6 pawn and d5 hole can survive into the endgame, so activity and timely piece exchanges must compensate for structural defects. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. Be3 Be6 10. Qd2 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 11. Rfd1 Rc8 12. Rac1 { Rac1: White places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Control d5, activate rooks, and exchange the white knight that can occupy the outpost. White plan: White fixes d6, installs a knight on d5, and trades defenders to create a long-term squeeze. Decision: Which piece is White's ideal occupier of d5, and can Black exchange it before simplification? Danger: Entering an endgame because queens are gone while leaving the perfect blockading knight untouched. Memory rule: In an ...e5 ending, control the outpost before counting the pawns. } *

