{ Volume 18: A Lifetime Najdorf Repertoire. Why it matters: The repertoire backbone links development, ...b5, control of d5, the c-file, and a central break into one causal chain. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 7. f3 b5 8. Qd2 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. g4 h6 10. O-O-O Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 11. Kb1 Rc8 { Rc8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Reach a known structure, identify White's attack, and choose the move that changes the race rather than merely following a memorized sequence. White plan: White varies move order to make Black reveal commitments or lose a tempo in the attack. Decision: Which move is essential to the position, and which move belongs only to the memorized order? Danger: Trying to reproduce a line after White has changed one detail that alters the tactics. Memory rule: Store structures and decisions; use move order only to reach them safely. } *

