[Variation "Anti-Sicilian decision tree"]

{ Volume 18: A Lifetime Najdorf Repertoire. Why it matters: Anti-Sicilians ask Black to identify White's intended center quickly: immediate d4, delayed d4, f4 attack, or gambit development. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. Bb5+ Bd7 4. Bxd7+ Qxd7 { Qxd7: Black releases tension by capturing on d7; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O Nc6 6. c3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 7. Re1 g6 8. d4 { d4: White builds the Open Sicilian center and offers the d-pawn for rapid development. Black plan: Classify the center, choose the matching break, and develop without importing Najdorf moves into the wrong structure. White plan: White avoids main-line theory to obtain a familiar setup and practical initiative. Decision: What pawn break is White preparing, and which black move challenges it most directly? Danger: Feeling relieved to be out of theory and responding with generic moves instead of a concrete plan. Memory rule: Name White's center first; the anti-Sicilian label comes second. } *

