[Variation "Repairing the repertoire from your games"]

{ Volume 18: A Lifetime Najdorf Repertoire. Why it matters: A lifetime repertoire grows from recurring decisions in your games, not from collecting every database branch at once. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 { e5: Black gains central space and a knight tempo while accepting a lasting weakness on d5. } 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. f4 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: After each game, find the first position you could not explain, record the cue and candidate set, then repair only that branch. White plan: Opponents will choose sidelines and imperfect moves; those are tests of understanding rather than failures of preparation. Decision: At what move did your reasoning disappear, and what one decision rule would restore it next time? Danger: Replacing the whole opening after one loss or adding hundreds of moves without retrieval practice. Memory rule: Repair the first unexplained decision, then test it again. } *

